WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:05.200
Transport qualification is evidence under stated conditions, not a guarantee against every

2
00:00:05.240 --> 00:00:05.540
journey.

3
00:00:06.360 --> 00:00:11.340
The twenty twenty-six transport-vibration discussion highlights why motion in multiple directions and

4
00:00:11.380 --> 00:00:13.120
interactions among equipment matter.

5
00:00:13.720 --> 00:00:17.260
A single test result should be read with its load, fixtures, input

6
00:00:17.280 --> 00:00:19.220
conditions and acceptance criteria.

7
00:00:19.840 --> 00:00:23.020
Ask whether the tested configuration represents the proposed shipment.

8
00:00:23.560 --> 00:00:29.280
Changes to equipment population, restraints, accessories, support or packaging can affect applicability.

9
00:00:30.040 --> 00:00:33.840
Also distinguish a frame test from evidence for the complete populated rack.

10
00:00:34.300 --> 00:00:38.520
The frame may survive while a connection or installed assembly becomes unacceptable.

11
00:00:39.320 --> 00:00:43.160
Use the Summit material to sharpen the questions, not to invent universal

12
00:00:43.240 --> 00:00:43.820
shipping limits.

13
00:00:44.420 --> 00:00:48.720
Request the applicable qualification record and the supplier's approved handling conditions.

14
00:00:49.240 --> 00:00:52.660
Define receiving and post-move inspections against those conditions.

15
00:00:53.180 --> 00:00:57.640
If the route or configuration lies outside the supported envelope, escalate before

16
00:00:57.660 --> 00:00:58.300
moving the rack.

17
00:00:59.040 --> 00:01:02.760
A successful previous shipment is useful experience, but it doesn't close a

18
00:01:02.780 --> 00:01:04.680
material difference in the next deployment.

